sdio: give artosyn_read() the same real (patient) wait 0012 already gave
artosyn_sdio_write(), instead of the same 10*10ms msleep-and-repoll
pattern 0012's own commit message diagnosed and fixed on the write side

Depends on 0010/0011/0012 (numbered after them; same function family
0012 already touched, purely on the read side this time).

Background: while root-causing the SDIO-mode "bb_socket_write made no
progress" throughput stalls (see this project's ar8030-transport repo,
tx/main.c and its README's "SDIO chardev" section), an isolation test
swapping the vendor's real artosyn_sdio.ko kernel module in (keeping
this project's own daemon, including its 0013-*.patch fix) eliminated
the stalls entirely -- proving the bug lives in this driver's own
read/write/interrupt-handling logic, not the daemon. Re-reading
artosyn_read() with that narrowed focus found it had never received
0012's own fix: it still runs the exact same anti-pattern 0012's commit
message already diagnosed for artosyn_sdio_write() -- a `do { ... }
while()` loop checking artosyn_sdio_read_condition(), sleeping 10ms and
re-queuing the WORKAROUND_FOR_INTERRUPT_LOST_ISSUE reg_workqueue between
checks, giving up after 10 retries (100ms total) with -EIO. There is
even a commented-out `wait_event_interruptible_timeout(dev->rx_q,
artosyn_sdio_read_condition(dev), 10)` call immediately above the loop,
abandoned in favor of the cruder retry loop for reasons lost to history
-- the same shape of abandoned attempt 0012 found and replaced on the
write side.

In this project's current call pattern this doesn't appear to be the
dominant cause of the remaining stalls: sdio_ev_loop() (daemon/dev8030/
sdio8030/sdio_dev.c) only ever calls read() on this chardev after
poll() has already reported POLLIN ready, so artosyn_sdio_read_condition()
is almost always already true by the time artosyn_read() runs, and the
old loop (like the new wait) returns on its very first check either way
-- confirmed the isolation test's stalls aren't explained by this
alone. But it's still the same fragile, capped-at-100ms-total,
unbounded-latency-under-a-genuinely-missed-real-interrupt pattern 0012
already found real hardware evidence against on the write side, left
untouched here for no principled reason -- fixed the same way, for
consistency and as defense-in-depth against exactly the missed-interrupt
scenario 0012's own analysis covers, while the actual remaining root
cause of the stalls stays open (see the README's own "Isolation test
and the real root cause" section for what's still unexplained).

